<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The resource-server pipeline contract, as something a deployment can reference.

    WHY THIS EXISTS, AND IT IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A HUNCH. On 2026-08-26 the first consumer
    outside this repository wired a resource server, ran a suite of 402 tests green, and then found
    by hand - with curl, against a running process - that the RFC 9728 document answered 401 at the
    exact URL that server's own challenges pointed clients at. Two more of the same shape came with
    it. Not one was reachable from a unit test, because not one of them is about a unit: they are
    about what a wired pipeline answers, and every one of them presents to a client as "OAuth is
    broken" rather than as "an endpoint is behind the wrong middleware".

    So the contract takes an HttpClient over a running pipeline and asks the questions a client
    asks. A deployment derives it, supplies its own wired app, and finds out before its users do.

    The contract deliberately spells the well-known suffix itself rather than reading this
    library's constant. A conformance check that shares a constant with the code under test agrees
    with that constant's bugs; a client builds the URL from RFC 9728 and so does this.

    No test runner here, on purpose - the same rule as Boltway.Interaction.Testing. This assembly is
    never executed by itself; whichever project derives from it supplies the runner, and adding one
    would put a second copy in their build.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>false</IsTestProject>
    <Description>
      The resource-server pipeline contract, as an xunit base class. Supply an HttpClient over your
      wired application and the suite checks what a client actually meets: both RFC 9728 well-known
      forms answering without a credential, the challenge naming a metadata URL that is really
      there, and a bad token producing a 401 rather than a 403 or a 500.
    </Description>

    <!--
      CA1707 off for the same reason as the interaction contracts: a contract method's name is what
      a consumer reads in their own runner when it fails, so
      `The_metadata_url_named_in_the_challenge_is_reachable` is the finding. CS1591 stays on,
      because this is an API surface a consumer derives from.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1707</NoWarn>

    <!--
      OFF UNTIL THIS ID HAS SHIPPED ONCE, AND THIS LINE COMES OUT IN THE RELEASE AFTER 0.4.0.

      Package validation diffs a pack against PackageValidationBaselineVersion, which is the release
      before this one. There is no such release of this id, so the restore fails with NU1101 - the
      gate asking for a baseline that cannot exist. Every other packable project here keeps it on.

      The condition for removing it is exactly one publish: once 0.4.0 is on the feed, delete these
      lines and the family default applies, and from then on a removed or re-signatured member of
      this contract fails the pack like everywhere else. Leaving it here longer means the one
      assembly a consumer derives from is the one assembly nothing watches for breaks.
    -->
    <EnablePackageValidation>false</EnablePackageValidation>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The assertion library only. Never the runner, never the collector. -->
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    NO PROJECT REFERENCE, and that is the design rather than an omission.

    Everything below is asked over HTTP with BCL types, so the contract compiles against nothing of
    this product's. A deployment can therefore run it against a resource server built on something
    else entirely - which is the honest test of whether these assertions are about RFC 9728 or about
    Boltway. They are about RFC 9728.
  -->

</Project>
